            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "SunnySrivastava",
                        "email": "sunnsr25@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SunnySrivastava1984"
                    },
                    "message": "The change should be applicable to all the services implementing the interface, not restricted to PIM?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vankayala Rekha Aparna",
                        "email": "vrekhaaparna@ibm.com",
                        "username": "RekhaAparna01"
                    },
                    "message": "Updated"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you provide an example?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vankayala Rekha Aparna",
                        "email": "vrekhaaparna@ibm.com",
                        "username": "RekhaAparna01"
                    },
                    "message": "One of the use case can be that a PCIe slot may support multiple types of cards and depending  on the type of card present it may expose different child objects, such as connector/ports. So, when a card is replaced with a different type, depending upon the connector/ports exposed by the replaced card the subtree can change. Existing inventory objects and their persisted data must be removed to prevent stale entries from remaining in the D-Bus inventory tree."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you make a full example with the services and hardware involved."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Can https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/blob/master/yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Object/Delete.interface.yaml be used instead?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Then that interface would have to be on every single path in the tree, which there can be a ton of."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "SunnySrivastava",
                        "email": "sunnsr25@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SunnySrivastava1984"
                    },
                    "message": "@spinler@us.ibm.com +1, Every object will have to implement that interface, with this API in place the manager hosting the object can be directly requested to remove the required Object."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Why does every object have to impleement it if PIM already knows for which objects this is applicable? It can create a type for removable objects and implement Object.Delete."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "SunnySrivastava",
                        "email": "sunnsr25@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SunnySrivastava1984"
                    },
                    "message": ">  Why does every object have to impleement it if PIM already knows for which objects this is applicable?\n\n\na) The interface description says that it deletes the object that implements delete. \nb) There are multiple objects hosted under PIM, if only certain object(s) needs to be deleted, how PIM can be notified by the caller which object(s) to delete? \n\nHaving a DeleteObject method exposed at PIM level gives the flexibility to call that API with specific object path(s) that needs to be removed.\n\nI am sorry if I am missing something but I am not able to join to dots how the interface suggested above can help here.\nCould you please elaborate more on that part."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "SunnySrivastava",
                        "email": "sunnsr25@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SunnySrivastava1984"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we please make some progress on this"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jinu Joy Thomas",
                        "email": "jinu.joy.thomas@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "jinuthomas"
                    },
                    "message": "I do not see any issue of having a DeleteObject. But just curious as to, why do we not go for a dict , so that multiple paths can be deleted at a shot instead of calling it singly multiple time ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This seems to be the fundamental discussion here:\n\n---\n\nDeepak:\n> Can [xyz.openbmc_project.Object.Delete] be used instead.\n\nMatt:\n> Then that interface would have to be on every single path in the tree, which there can be a ton of.\n\n---\n\n@spinler@us.ibm.com - It isn't obvious to me why \"there can be a ton of\" is a problem.  You can have a lot of inventory objects, perhaps, but adding the Object.Delete interface is as simple as changing the inherit for the base class, right?\n\nAre we trying to pursue a memory optimization?  If so, do we have numbers on what the savings is?\n\nGenerally we have Common interfaces so ... people use them.  I'm not saying we can't have a new function here but I'm just trying to understand what the engineering constraint is that we're solving by adding it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I suspect it'll be over a thousand, but then again the logging namespace does the same thing so I will withdraw my comment about the number of them.\n\nAs far as actually implementing that, I'm not sure how hard that would be."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Which service(s) are the users of API?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vankayala Rekha Aparna",
                        "email": "vrekhaaparna@ibm.com",
                        "username": "RekhaAparna01"
                    },
                    "message": "It can be used by service(s) which can detect presence/absence of any given inventory item and needs to update the inventory accordingly."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Which service(s) are the users of API?\n\n> It can be used by service(s) which can detect presence/absence of any \ngiven inventory item and needs to update the inventory accordingly.\n\nI don't think you answered Deepak's question here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Manager.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "SunnySrivastava",
                        "email": "sunnsr25@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SunnySrivastava1984"
                    },
                    "message": "@alexander.hansen@9elements.com At this moment it is difficult to list down all the service(s) that can be user(s) of this API.\n\nAs stated above, this can be used by any service which intend to modify the listed object paths managed by PIM.\nIf there is an API to add an object, there should also be an API to remove the same else addition becomes a one time irrevocable action."
                },
                {
